{
  "image_name": "fd9f41ae7fe337b2c55a6d988a14b964.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a progression from the classic Leyden jar to modern structural, load-bearing capacitors. It begins by contrasting a glass-based capacitor, where the dielectric does not allow ionic motion and is therefore rigid and brittle, with conventional supercapacitors that use a liquid electrolyte, enabling charge storage with mobile ions and mechanical deformability. The central idea is a newer load-bearing capacitor that replaces the liquid with a polymer solid electrolyte, aiming to combine electrochemical function with structural integrity.\n\nThe chemistry shown suggests an ion-conducting solid polymer system built from epoxy-type and isocyanate-containing components together with lithium and ionic-liquid salts, producing a material that can both transport ions and support load. A schematic of the polymer electrolyte emphasizes this dual role: soft, interconnected regions serve as pathways for ion transport, while a separate phase provides mechanical strength.\n\nThe final schematic extends this concept into a multilayer composite device. Carbon fibers and glass fibers are embedded with the solid electrolyte between layers, indicating a structural laminate that stores charge while acting as part of the load-bearing material. Overall, the figure highlights the trade-off and proposed integration of energy storage and mechanical performance.",
